WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.056 --> 00:00:03.467
The voyage began as many such voyages do. The air quivered with excitement, adventure, and a sense

2
00:00:03.468 --> 00:00:12.562
of mystery as my fellow passengers and I boarded the transatlantic steamship Provence . We enjoyed making

3
00:00:12.563 --> 00:00:22.610
each other's acquaintance, and though we were sociable and charming, we kept the safe distance that characterizes

4
00:00:22.611 --> 00:00:31.039
those who may never see each other again. Here, on this ship, we had the luxury of being

5
00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:36.777
isolated from the world, except for one inescapable link—the wireless telegraph. Its voice followed us around like

6
00:00:36.778 --> 00:00:44.507
an ever-present shadow, whispering words of the outside world. I was the recipient of two telegrams. Others,

7
00:00:44.508 --> 00:00:54.007
too, received messages from family and friends wishing them bon voyage . However, on the second day

8
00:00:54.008 --> 00:00:59.682
of our voyage, we received a telegram so startling and vicious, it destroyed the peace we had

9
00:00:59.683 --> 00:01:07.150
enjoyed up until that moment. It read: Caution! Arsène Lupin is aboard the Provence. First-class cabin, blond

10
00:01:07.151 --> 00:01:14.745
hair, wound on right forearm. Traveling alone under the name of R— At that most crucial moment,

11
00:01:14.746 --> 00:01:19.650
a flash of lightning interrupted the transmission, and the rest of the message never reached us. The passengers

12
00:01:19.651 --> 00:01:25.404
only knew Lupin was using an alias beginning with the letter R . That, however, was enough

13
00:01:25.405 --> 00:01:35.142
to rouse fear and apprehension among everyone on board the ship. Arsène Lupin! The infamous gentleman burglar's

14
00:01:35.143 --> 00:01:47.134
exploits were well reported in newspapers across France and in other European countries. "Horrible! Arsène Lupin is

15
00:01:47.135 --> 00:01:57.801
in our midst!" exclaimed Miss Nelly Underdown, the prettiest woman on the ship and—dare I say—loveliest of

16
00:01:57.802 --> 00:02:03.285
all lovely creatures on land. "They say Lupin is a man of a thousand faces," I said.

17
00:02:03.286 --> 00:02:12.761
"He could appear as a chauffeur or a Russian physician, a Spanish bullfighter or an old man.

18
00:02:12.762 --> 00:02:18.102
No one can say for certain what Lupin truly looks like. He is a master of disguise." "You

19
00:02:18.103 --> 00:02:24.753
do not put one's mind at ease," lamented Lady Jerland, who was Miss Nelly's traveling companion. "How

20
00:02:24.754 --> 00:02:29.602
are we to secure our valuables?" A group of us had gathered on the rear deck of

21
00:02:29.603 --> 00:02:39.404
the ship, reclining comfortably on lounge chairs. The day was bright and warm, with no sign of

22
00:02:39.405 --> 00:02:47.475
the storm that had interrupted the notorious telegram. "How can we protect ourselves from this madman?" cried

23
00:02:47.476 --> 00:02:51.824
Miss Nelly. "I would hardly call Lupin a madman," I said, trying to comfort her and gently

24
00:02:51.825 --> 00:03:02.229
placing my hand on her shoulder. She lowered her eyes and blushed. "Monsieur d'Andrézy," said Miss Nelly,

25
00:03:02.230 --> 00:03:10.626
"you are on such intimate terms with the captain. Surely you know something?" I laughed, delighted by Miss

26
00:03:10.627 --> 00:03:14.047
Nelly's confidence in me. "I assure you, I have no definite knowledge," I replied. "But can't we

27
00:03:14.048 --> 00:03:24.372
investigate this matter just as well as Detective Ganimard, the personal enemy of Arsène Lupin?" It was

28
00:03:24.373 --> 00:03:36.380
common knowledge that Ganimard had vowed to bring Lupin to justice, even if he had to follow

29
00:03:36.381 --> 00:03:47.864
Lupin to the ends of the earth. "It sounds a bit complicated," said Miss Nelly. "Lupin's vocation,

30
00:03:47.865 --> 00:03:54.023
though unsavory to many, does take a certain amount of skill. Every movement detailed and timed to

31
00:03:54.024 --> 00:04:00.341
the second—" "If I may be so bold as to speak," interrupted Monsieur Rozaine, a young man

32
00:04:00.342 --> 00:04:08.174
who rivaled me for the affections of Miss Nelly earlier, "it appears that you admire Lupin, Monsieur Bernard

33
00:04:08.175 --> 00:04:17.412
d'Andrézy." Ignoring his comment, I continued. "Lupin's skill demonstrates not the work of a madman, Miss Nelly,

34
00:04:17.413 --> 00:04:22.253
but the art of a clever, organized, and very meticulous person." "But then who could ever catch

35
00:04:22.254 --> 00:04:32.055
such a man?" Miss Nelly sighed, her full lips in a pout. I took a moment to

36
00:04:32.056 --> 00:04:36.499
reflect on the exquisite Miss Nelly, with her golden hair and deep emerald eyes. Her skin was

37
00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:41.888
like porcelain, and her body was as perfectly proportioned as that of Venus, the goddess of beauty.

38
00:04:41.889 --> 00:04:48.118
"My dear, all this dreadful talk has left me quite weak," Lady Jerland murmured. "I shall retire to

39
00:04:48.119 --> 00:04:53.539
my quarters to lie down before tea." I waited patiently until Lady Jerland left before going on.

40
00:04:53.540 --> 00:04:57.626
"Although we might not be quite as resourceful as the celebrated Detective Ganimard, we do have some

41
00:04:57.627 --> 00:05:00.896
facts to help us determine who Lupin may be." "Please explain, Monsieur d'Andrézy," pleaded Miss Nelly. "With

42
00:05:00.897 --> 00:05:04.977
pleasure," I said, bowing, and removed a passenger list from my right pocket. Using my left hand

43
00:05:04.978 --> 00:05:10.206
to unfold the paper took a few moments, and everyone patiently waited for me to resume. "First,

44
00:05:10.207 --> 00:05:13.952
Lupin is using an alias beginning with R ," I reminded them. "That's a common letter for

45
00:05:13.953 --> 00:05:17.031
a surname," argued Rozaine. "Second, he is traveling alone," I noted. "So are half the passengers on this

46
00:05:17.032 --> 00:05:29.364
ship," Rozaine interjected. Miss Nelly nodded vigorously in agreement, and Rozaine's face lit up. "Third, he is

47
00:05:29.365 --> 00:05:33.864
blond." I smiled at everyone. "Well?" Rozaine shrugged his shoulders. "Well, now it is a matter of

48
00:05:33.865 --> 00:05:40.928
process of elimination," I said. "Here, on this passenger list, there are only thirteen men with surnames

49
00:05:40.929 --> 00:05:46.412
that begin with R , and nine are family men." "That leaves just four who are traveling

50
00:05:46.413 --> 00:05:51.166
alone," calculated Miss Nelly. "Correct, mademoiselle," I nodded. "Of the four, one is the Marquis de Raverdan—"

51
00:05:51.167 --> 00:05:57.158
"He is the secretary to the American ambassador," interrupted Miss Nelly. "I know him well." I scrolled

52
00:05:57.159 --> 00:06:01.745
down the list. "The second is Major Rawson." "He's my uncle," someone else called out. "Monsieur Rivolta is

53
00:06:01.746 --> 00:06:05.563
the third," I said. "That's me," exclaimed a dark Italian with a heavy, black beard. "You can

54
00:06:05.564 --> 00:06:10.246
hardly be called a blond," said Miss Nelly. "And finally"—I paused before continuing—"Monsieur Rozaine." All eyes fixed

55
00:06:10.247 --> 00:06:16.984
on the young, blond-haired man. He turned red and was rendered speechless. "Well, Monsieur Rozaine? Have you

56
00:06:16.985 --> 00:06:22.222
anything to say?" Miss Nelly demanded of him. "This is insanity!" Rozaine finally cried. He struggled to

57
00:06:22.223 --> 00:06:30.126
his feet. Some of the women recoiled in terror. "I admit I fit the description of that

58
00:06:30.127 --> 00:06:38.039
scoundrel Lupin, but I swear I am not he," Rozaine shouted excitedly. "Didn't the telegram say something

59
00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:42.626
about a wound?" asked Miss Nelly. Everyone nodded and began murmuring. "I can prove to you I have

60
00:06:42.627 --> 00:06:45.213
no such wound," sputtered Rozaine, rolling up his left shirtsleeve. Smiling secretly, I remembered the wound was

61
00:06:45.214 --> 00:06:51.539
said to be on his right arm, not on his left. I was about to call attention

62
00:06:51.540 --> 00:06:58.206
to that fact when a loud scream erupted from the sleeping quarters. Everyone abandoned the deck and

63
00:06:58.207 --> 00:07:04.610
rushed to the inner part of the ship. "Help, help!" Lady Jerland wailed frantically. Her cheeks were

64
00:07:04.611 --> 00:07:08.698
smeared with makeup and tears. She was breathing heavily. "They're gone! My jewels! My pearls! They're gone!"

65
00:07:08.699 --> 00:07:22.103
I led the way into Lady Jerland's cabin. Pieces of jewelry still lay on top of her

66
00:07:22.104 --> 00:07:37.833
dresser; the thief had taken only the most precious diamonds and pearls and left the rest. Each stolen

67
00:07:37.834 --> 00:07:44.254
jewel had been carefully removed from its mounting. "Yes, Lady Jerland's unblemished pearls were indeed a prize,"

68
00:07:44.255 --> 00:07:48.815
I whispered to Miss Nelly. "Who could have done this?" Lady Jerland wept bitterly. The passengers looked

69
00:07:48.816 --> 00:07:57.935
at one another. They had all reached the same conclusion. This was the work of Arsène Lupin.

